that the geometric series

493
00:37:23,220 --> 00:37:35,900
is convergent if c here in
absolute value is less than 1

494
00:37:35,900 --> 00:37:49,700
and divergent otherwise,
if the absolute value of c

495
00:37:49,700 --> 00:37:52,140
is bigger than 1.

496
00:37:52,140 --> 00:37:54,970
So this is the claim that--

497
00:37:57,580 --> 00:38:00,180
and so this is a claim.

498
00:38:00,180 --> 00:38:10,696
And in fact, so one
more thing is that--

499
00:38:14,700 --> 00:38:29,460
so moreover, if c is less
than 1, then the limit--

500
00:38:29,460 --> 00:38:36,660
so then the claim is that it's
convergent-- then the limit

501
00:38:36,660 --> 00:38:41,700
is 1 minus c.

502
00:38:41,700 --> 00:38:42,880
This is the limit.

503
00:38:42,880 --> 00:38:48,210
And so we write this here
as this infinite sum.

504
00:38:53,260 --> 00:38:56,940
Now, let's just
try to prove this.

505
00:38:56,940 --> 00:39:02,660
So let me first do-- and
we're almost there already.

506
00:39:02,660 --> 00:39:05,760
So the thing is that--

507
00:39:14,430 --> 00:39:16,300
so let's first look at the case.

508
00:39:21,310 --> 00:39:29,070
Assume c in absolute value
is strictly less than 1.

509
00:39:29,070 --> 00:39:36,030
But then we have already
proven that sn is-- so sn,

510
00:39:36,030 --> 00:39:41,310
this here was the sum from
i equal to 0 to n of ci.

511
00:39:41,310 --> 00:39:46,470
And we have already proven that
this is 1 minus cn plus 1 over 1

512
00:39:46,470 --> 00:39:48,990
minus c.

513
00:39:48,990 --> 00:39:51,350
And this makes sense.

514
00:39:51,350 --> 00:39:53,130
Norm is strictly less than 1.

515
00:39:53,130 --> 00:39:55,290
So in particular,
c is not 1, right?

516
00:39:55,290 --> 00:39:57,710
So this here is fine.

517
00:39:57,710 --> 00:39:59,670
But now you see that
if you take something

518
00:39:59,670 --> 00:40:04,430
with norm strictly less
than 1, then just because cn

519
00:40:04,430 --> 00:40:10,190
plus 1, the norm of this here,
but if you take something

520
00:40:10,190 --> 00:40:14,630
and you multiply it by another--
so if you take two numbers, a

521
00:40:14,630 --> 00:40:17,630
and b, and you take
the absolute value

522
00:40:17,630 --> 00:40:20,430
of the product, that's
the same as the product

523
00:40:20,430 --> 00:40:23,310
of the absolute values.

524
00:40:23,310 --> 00:40:27,990
So this means that if you
take c to the power n plus 1--

525
00:40:27,990 --> 00:40:29,950
so if you take c
and you multiply it

526
00:40:29,950 --> 00:40:33,670
by itself n plus 1
time, this is the same

527
00:40:33,670 --> 00:40:37,030
as the absolute value
times [INAUDIBLE] raised

528
00:40:37,030 --> 00:40:39,650
to the power n plus 1 right.

529
00:40:39,650 --> 00:40:42,710
And so now you see
that because this c has

530
00:40:42,710 --> 00:40:46,950
absolute value less than
1, strictly less than 1,

531
00:40:46,950 --> 00:40:52,350
then as n goes to infinity,
this here converges to 0.

532
00:40:52,350 --> 00:40:55,910
So this means that as
n goes to infinity,

533
00:40:55,910 --> 00:40:59,150
this thing here might converge.

534
00:40:59,150 --> 00:41:00,870
This here goes to 0.

535
00:41:00,870 --> 00:41:05,270
So just by the algebraic
rules for limits,

536
00:41:05,270 --> 00:41:10,670
this thing here is going to
converge to 1 minus c over 1,

537
00:41:10,670 --> 00:41:11,430
like that.

538
00:41:14,710 --> 00:41:20,830
So you see that in the case
where c in absolute value

539
00:41:20,830 --> 00:41:23,790
is strictly less than
1, then we have now

540
00:41:23,790 --> 00:41:26,810
proven that the geometric
series is convergent.

541
00:41:26,810 --> 00:41:30,230
And in fact, we have even,
which is really nice,

542
00:41:30,230 --> 00:41:34,090
and unusual for a series.

543
00:41:34,090 --> 00:41:37,190
I mean, usually you cannot
actually determine what

544
00:41:37,190 --> 00:41:38,270
the limit is.

545
00:41:38,270 --> 00:41:39,790
But in this case, we could.

546
00:41:39,790 --> 00:41:43,070
But we still need
to figure out why

547
00:41:43,070 --> 00:41:51,790
is it not convergent if
the absolute value of c

548
00:41:51,790 --> 00:41:54,710
is bigger or equal to 1.

549
00:41:54,710 --> 00:41:58,350
And so here's an easy thing.

550
00:41:58,350 --> 00:42:00,950
I mean, but you could also--

551
00:42:00,950 --> 00:42:04,710
yeah, so here's an easy thing,
that if you're looking at c--

552
00:42:04,710 --> 00:42:14,590
sorry, sn plus 1 minus sn,
where sn plus 1 or sn say,

553
00:42:14,590 --> 00:42:23,850
this here was just c to the
power 0 up to c to the power n.

554
00:42:23,850 --> 00:42:28,950
Sn plus 1 is where
you do the same stuff.

555
00:42:28,950 --> 00:42:32,190
We're adding these guys.

556
00:42:32,190 --> 00:42:36,630
But then you have to
add one more, right?

557
00:42:36,630 --> 00:42:40,230
So this means that this
difference here is really

558
00:42:40,230 --> 00:42:45,190
c to the n plus 1, right?

559
00:42:45,190 --> 00:42:47,990
But now I could look
at the absolute value

560
00:42:47,990 --> 00:42:50,670
of this difference.

561
00:42:50,670 --> 00:42:53,790
This is this.

562
00:42:53,790 --> 00:43:00,470
But this is the same, again,
as c to the power n plus 1.

563
00:43:00,470 --> 00:43:05,470
But you see, if c here is
the absolute value is bigger

564
00:43:05,470 --> 00:43:09,070
or equal to 1, then
this thing here-- oops--

565
00:43:09,070 --> 00:43:12,630
then this thing here, so
if the absolute value here

566
00:43:12,630 --> 00:43:16,070
is bigger or equal to
1, then this thing here

567
00:43:16,070 --> 00:43:19,630
must be bigger or equal to 1.

568
00:43:19,630 --> 00:43:23,290
Remember that if a
sequence is convergent,

569
00:43:23,290 --> 00:43:27,830
if a sequence is convergent,
then it is a Cauchy sequence.

570
00:43:27,830 --> 00:43:31,430
It's a Cauchy sequence that
if you go sufficiently far

571
00:43:31,430 --> 00:43:36,710
out, then from there on and
out, everything bunch very, very

572
00:43:36,710 --> 00:43:37,890
close together.

573
00:43:37,890 --> 00:43:41,230
But you see here, not
even two consecutive

574
00:43:41,230 --> 00:43:44,990
is bunching together, because
independent on how far,

575
00:43:44,990 --> 00:43:49,110
how large n is, the difference
is always at least 1.

576
00:43:49,110 --> 00:43:51,110
So this is not even a Cauchy--

577
00:43:51,110 --> 00:43:53,850
I mean, well, it's actually
equivalent, of course,

578
00:43:53,850 --> 00:43:55,370
but it's not a Cauchy sequence.

579
00:43:55,370 --> 00:43:56,910
It's failed.

580
00:43:56,910 --> 00:44:00,470
Like the most basic thing
with consecutive ones

581
00:44:00,470 --> 00:44:03,870
does not even lie
close together.

582
00:44:03,870 --> 00:44:09,550
So this proves this theorem
about the geometric convergence,

583
00:44:09,550 --> 00:44:12,630
the geometric
series convergence.

584
00:44:12,630 --> 00:44:23,750
So this is the most important
example of a sequence.

585
00:44:23,750 --> 00:44:28,270
Maybe the second
most important is

586
00:44:28,270 --> 00:44:35,920
what's called the harmonic
series, so harmonic series.

587
00:44:44,750 --> 00:44:48,830
But the harmonic series is
unfortunately not a series

588
00:44:48,830 --> 00:44:50,670
that is convergent.

589
00:44:50,670 --> 00:44:52,770
So the harmonic series
is the following.

590
00:44:52,770 --> 00:44:54,150
So again, I'm writing it--

591
00:44:54,150 --> 00:44:59,440
I'm using this abusive notation
that I'm writing the series as

592
00:44:59,440 --> 00:45:01,280
like an infinite sum.

593
00:45:01,280 --> 00:45:06,540
But this infinite sum, but
it's not a convergent series,

594
00:45:06,540 --> 00:45:08,920
so the infinite sum
doesn't really make sense.

595
00:45:08,920 --> 00:45:14,460
So here I have to start
because I'm dividing by n.

596
00:45:14,460 --> 00:45:16,560
So I'm starting by 1 with 1.

597
00:45:16,560 --> 00:45:19,760
So this is called
the harmonic series.

598
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:20,300
OK.

599
00:45:20,300 --> 00:45:31,280
And so for series,
there's a bunch

600
00:45:31,280 --> 00:45:34,120
of things, a bunch
of tests that are

601
00:45:34,120 --> 00:45:37,420
useful to determine whether a
series is convergent or not.

602
00:45:41,720 --> 00:45:43,640
And we will talk
about these tests.

603
00:45:43,640 --> 00:45:45,980
I'll talk a little bit
about later in this lecture.

604
00:45:45,980 --> 00:45:48,820
I'll talk about some of the
tests, but not all of them.

605
00:45:48,820 --> 00:45:51,760
And we will later
talk about more.

606
00:45:51,760 --> 00:45:55,020
The test that is particularly
useful in this case,

607
00:45:55,020 --> 00:45:56,580
it's what's called
the integral test.

608
00:45:56,580 --> 00:45:59,760
But I won't talk
about that today.

609
00:45:59,760 --> 00:46:05,560
But instead, I will show you
a very simple proof of why

610
00:46:05,560 --> 00:46:09,020
this here is not convergent.

611
00:46:12,000 --> 00:46:15,750
So here's a theorem.

612
00:46:19,920 --> 00:46:30,162
The harmonic series
is a divergence.

613
00:46:30,162 --> 00:46:31,120
So it's not convergent.

614
00:46:31,120 --> 00:46:32,100
It's divergent.

615
00:46:34,680 --> 00:46:37,000
And so in fact,
what we will prove

616
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:44,080
is the following, that we will
show that if you're looking at--

617
00:46:44,080 --> 00:46:46,240
so n is an integer.

618
00:46:46,240 --> 00:46:48,820
If you're looking at--

619
00:46:48,820 --> 00:46:51,200
so this is like--

620
00:46:51,200 --> 00:46:57,920
so this is only some of the--
you only hit, for this here,

621
00:46:57,920 --> 00:47:03,200
you only hit some of the
elements in the sequence.

622
00:47:03,200 --> 00:47:11,120
So this is where I'm summing
again from i equal to 1 to 2

623
00:47:11,120 --> 00:47:16,760
to the n minus 1, n is
an integer, of 1 over i.

624
00:47:16,760 --> 00:47:21,280
So I want to prove
that these guys here,

625
00:47:21,280 --> 00:47:24,900
if the original sequence
had been convergent,

626
00:47:24,900 --> 00:47:28,400
then of course, any subsequence
would also be convergent.

627
00:47:28,400 --> 00:47:32,440
And we're going to prove
that this thing here

628
00:47:32,440 --> 00:47:36,000
is bigger or equal to n over 2.

629
00:47:36,000 --> 00:47:37,820
So I claim that we have this.

630
00:47:37,820 --> 00:47:44,840
So this is a claim
that you have this.

631
00:47:44,840 --> 00:47:47,440
But if you have this
thing here, you see,

632
00:47:47,440 --> 00:47:51,340
that then this subsequence
here is unbounded.

633
00:47:51,340 --> 00:47:54,640
So of course, the
original harmonic series

634
00:47:54,640 --> 00:47:57,280
is not convergent.

635
00:47:57,280 --> 00:47:59,440
So let's try to prove this.

636
00:47:59,440 --> 00:48:06,320
Again, this is just like
a simple proof of that--

637
00:48:06,320 --> 00:48:09,280
very simple-minded proof--
that the harmonic series

638
00:48:09,280 --> 00:48:11,040
is divergent.

639
00:48:11,040 --> 00:48:12,953
But there are more.

640
00:48:15,960 --> 00:48:18,700
Once we learn about the
integral test and so on,

641
00:48:18,700 --> 00:48:24,520
there are other way
better ways of doing it.

642
00:48:24,520 --> 00:48:26,220
So let me try to prove this.

643
00:48:26,220 --> 00:48:31,310
So I want to prove the
claim, proof of the claim.

644
00:48:37,080 --> 00:48:38,860
So I'm going to prove it, right?

645
00:48:38,860 --> 00:48:40,440
So I just proved.

646
00:48:40,440 --> 00:48:43,440
So I want to establish
this for all n.

647
00:48:43,440 --> 00:48:51,960
And I'm going to prove it
first when n is equal to 1.

648
00:48:51,960 --> 00:48:55,920
So let's show this
for n equal to 1.

649
00:48:55,920 --> 00:49:05,100
So for n equal to 1, well then,
the claim here means that--

650
00:49:05,100 --> 00:49:07,600
so 2 to the 1, that's true.

651
00:49:07,600 --> 00:49:16,020
So this is s1, right, because
2 to the 1 is 2 minus 1.

652
00:49:16,020 --> 00:49:17,400
So this is 1.

653
00:49:17,400 --> 00:49:19,720
So I claim that.

654
00:49:19,720 --> 00:49:25,920
So I want to prove that s1
is bigger or equal to 1/2.

655
00:49:25,920 --> 00:49:26,860
[INAUDIBLE] to show.

656
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:36,280
But of course, s1 here,
s1 is just 1 over 1.

657
00:49:36,280 --> 00:49:40,040
So that's definitely fine.

658
00:49:40,040 --> 00:49:47,240
So this is-- so we want
to prove the claim.

659
00:49:47,240 --> 00:49:49,640
And the claim is OK.

660
00:49:49,640 --> 00:49:56,120
So claim is OK for n equal to 1.

661
00:49:56,120 --> 00:49:58,740
So now I want to prove it.

662
00:49:58,740 --> 00:50:15,160
So I want to assume the
claim is correct for n.

663
00:50:15,160 --> 00:50:22,440
And we want to prove
it for, want to show

664
00:50:22,440 --> 00:50:30,060
the claim for the next one.

665
00:50:30,060 --> 00:50:33,300
And so I have that.

666
00:50:33,300 --> 00:50:43,880
So we assume that s2n
minus 1, that this

667
00:50:43,880 --> 00:50:47,840
is bigger than n over 2.

668
00:50:47,840 --> 00:50:55,270
And then I want to look
at s2n plus 1 minus 1.

669
00:50:55,270 --> 00:50:58,450
OK, how can I write
this thing here?

670
00:50:58,450 --> 00:51:00,990
So I'm summing, right?

671
00:51:00,990 --> 00:51:04,290
This here is a sum of
the 1 over i, where

672
00:51:04,290 --> 00:51:06,690
I do it up to this thing here.

673
00:51:06,690 --> 00:51:10,330
But I can think about
that as the sum of the one

674
00:51:10,330 --> 00:51:14,530
where I do up to this here.

675
00:51:14,530 --> 00:51:20,210
And then I have to
sum the rest also.

676
00:51:20,210 --> 00:51:34,370
So this is i equal to 2n, to
2n plus 1, 2n plus 1 minus 1,

677
00:51:34,370 --> 00:51:38,090
like this, of 1 over i.

678
00:51:38,090 --> 00:51:47,000
But now you see, I want
a bounded from below.

679
00:51:47,000 --> 00:51:47,500
Fine.

680
00:51:50,090 --> 00:51:54,450
So if you continue this
line, then by assumption,

681
00:51:54,450 --> 00:51:58,730
this thing here is
bounded by n over 2.

682
00:51:58,730 --> 00:52:05,870
This thing here, I'm
summing 2n numbers.

683
00:52:05,870 --> 00:52:10,770
From here to here,
there are 2n numbers.

684
00:52:10,770 --> 00:52:16,570
And so it's 2n numbers.

685
00:52:16,570 --> 00:52:20,210
And if I take the smallest
of those two numbers,

686
00:52:20,210 --> 00:52:24,650
if I take the smallest of these
guys and I multiply it by 2n,

687
00:52:24,650 --> 00:52:26,510
then that's definitely
a lower bound.

688
00:52:26,510 --> 00:52:27,710
So let's try to do this.

689
00:52:27,710 --> 00:52:30,010
So I say 2n, 2 to the n--

690
00:52:30,010 --> 00:52:33,690
and then the smallest, but
the smallest is the last one.

691
00:52:33,690 --> 00:52:40,570
So the smallest is 1 over
2 to the n plus 1 minus 1.

692
00:52:40,570 --> 00:52:47,490
But this thing here is, of
course, obviously bigger

693
00:52:47,490 --> 00:52:51,590
than-- so n over 2, this thing
here-- well, this is true, then.

694
00:52:51,590 --> 00:52:55,650
But this thing here,
if I divide by a larger

695
00:52:55,650 --> 00:52:59,170
number and the larger
number is just this here,

696
00:52:59,170 --> 00:53:01,610
then I get something
even smaller.

697
00:53:01,610 --> 00:53:05,970
So I would have 1 over
2 to the n plus 1.

698
00:53:05,970 --> 00:53:11,250
But now you see that this thing
here cancel with these guys

699
00:53:11,250 --> 00:53:12,290
here.

700
00:53:12,290 --> 00:53:17,370
And so you're getting that
this is n over 2 plus 1/2.

701
00:53:17,370 --> 00:53:20,190
And so this is n plus 1 over 2.

702
00:53:20,190 --> 00:53:25,170
And that's exactly the
claim for n plus 1.

703
00:53:25,170 --> 00:53:28,490
So we're proven that if
it was true for n, then

704
00:53:28,490 --> 00:53:30,810
it's also true for n plus 1.

705
00:53:30,810 --> 00:53:37,330
And we remember to check that
the beginning was also OK.

706
00:53:37,330 --> 00:53:38,950
So that's a harmonic series.

707
00:53:38,950 --> 00:53:43,530
So you see that the harmonic
series is divergent.

708
00:53:43,530 --> 00:53:48,150
Now, the harmonic
series is, of course,

709
00:53:48,150 --> 00:53:54,810
an example of something else,
namely that if you're looking

710
00:53:54,810 --> 00:53:58,970
at-- so when you're
looking at a series,

711
00:53:58,970 --> 00:54:01,430
the first thing you
look at typically is--

712
00:54:07,650 --> 00:54:12,310
OK, so let me just
have a side here.

713
00:54:12,310 --> 00:54:17,490
So if you give me a series--

714
00:54:17,490 --> 00:54:19,370
it doesn't really matter where.

715
00:54:19,370 --> 00:54:24,290
I mean, it doesn't matter
very much where it's--

716
00:54:24,290 --> 00:54:27,190
for whether a series
is convergent,

717
00:54:27,190 --> 00:54:29,010
it doesn't matter
where you start,

718
00:54:29,010 --> 00:54:32,610
because this is just
adding something to it.

719
00:54:32,610 --> 00:54:34,910
It will matter where
you start for the limit,

720
00:54:34,910 --> 00:54:38,650
but not whether or not
the series is convergent.

721
00:54:38,650 --> 00:54:42,450
But if you give me a limit,
the first thing I always check

722
00:54:42,450 --> 00:54:44,230
is that this--

723
00:54:44,230 --> 00:54:59,420
so first, so for a series,
always check if an goes to 0

724
00:54:59,420 --> 00:54:59,920
or not.

725
00:55:02,930 --> 00:55:04,130
And why?

726
00:55:04,130 --> 00:55:09,210
Because if you give me a series,
the question about a series

727
00:55:09,210 --> 00:55:11,530
is always whether it's
converging or not.

728
00:55:11,530 --> 00:55:18,450
And again, just like we did,
if you're looking at sn, say,

729
00:55:18,450 --> 00:55:21,890
minus sn minus 1, this
is where I'm looking

730
00:55:21,890 --> 00:55:23,770
at two consecutive things.

731
00:55:23,770 --> 00:55:27,330
But if I'm looking at
this difference here,

732
00:55:27,330 --> 00:55:31,350
the difference here, this is
where I'm summing up to the a,

733
00:55:31,350 --> 00:55:32,930
n minus 1.

734
00:55:32,930 --> 00:55:35,050
Here I'm summing up to the an.

735
00:55:35,050 --> 00:55:38,290
So the difference here is an.

736
00:55:38,290 --> 00:55:40,170
And if the sequence--
just like we

737
00:55:40,170 --> 00:55:43,990
saw, if the sequence of
the series is convergent,

738
00:55:43,990 --> 00:55:47,150
it means that this sequence
here is convergent.

739
00:55:47,150 --> 00:55:50,050
But it means that it has to be
a Cauchy sequence, that things

740
00:55:50,050 --> 00:55:55,570
have to bunch together, but that
two consecutive bunch together

741
00:55:55,570 --> 00:55:58,630
is determined whether
an goes to 0 or not.

742
00:55:58,630 --> 00:56:03,350
So if an does not go to 0,
you're already done, right?

743
00:56:03,350 --> 00:56:05,210
There's no reason to continue.

744
00:56:05,210 --> 00:56:08,730
It's already clear that
if an does not go to 0,

745
00:56:08,730 --> 00:56:10,930
you can just say it
doesn't converge,

746
00:56:10,930 --> 00:56:12,590
because an doesn't go to 0.

747
00:56:12,590 --> 00:56:14,770
So there's no reason to
start with any kind of test

748
00:56:14,770 --> 00:56:18,640
or anything, any
sort of good ideas.

749
00:56:22,410 --> 00:56:25,610
So that was just a side remark.

750
00:56:25,610 --> 00:56:29,730
The other thing is that
for the harmonic series

751
00:56:29,730 --> 00:56:33,420
is-- so this, of course,
failed this test.

752
00:56:35,930 --> 00:56:42,340
But it also is an example of
something where you're taking--

753
00:56:42,340 --> 00:56:43,820
so suppose you take a--

754
00:56:43,820 --> 00:56:47,640
so you have a sequence, an.

755
00:56:47,640 --> 00:56:55,860
And then you're forming the
series, this series here.

756
00:56:55,860 --> 00:56:57,450
Now, if all of a n's--

757
00:57:00,190 --> 00:57:04,220
you post that all of the
a n's are non-negative,

758
00:57:04,220 --> 00:57:11,140
then if you're looking at sn
plus 1, you can think about sn

759
00:57:11,140 --> 00:57:18,500
plus 1 as where you're
summing the first n elements,

760
00:57:18,500 --> 00:57:22,680
and then you're adding
the n plus 1 element.

761
00:57:22,680 --> 00:57:30,260
So this here you can think
about as sn plus an plus 1.

762
00:57:30,260 --> 00:57:34,980
And so of course, if all of
these elements is non-negative,

763
00:57:34,980 --> 00:57:36,760
this means that this
has non-negative.

764
00:57:36,760 --> 00:57:39,700
So this thing here
is bigger than sn.

765
00:57:39,700 --> 00:57:43,340
So you see that if all of the
element, an, is non-negative,

766
00:57:43,340 --> 00:57:47,640
then this sequence,
sn, is monotone.

767
00:57:47,640 --> 00:57:49,500
So in this case here,
